              </ol>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the scalar and vector projections of <m>\vec{a}</m> onto <m>\vec{b}</m>.
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>\vec{a} = \langle 3, 6, -2 \rangle</m> and <m>\vec{b} = \langle 4, 5, 6 \rangle</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>\vec{a} = \langle -2, 3, -4 \rangle</m> and <m>\vec{b} = \langle 5, -6, 7 \rangle</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
       </exercises>
</subsection>


<subsection xml:id="subsec-cross_product">
  <title>Cross Product of Vectors</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      The cross product of two vectors is a vector that is perpendicular to both of the original vectors.
      It is denoted by <m>\vec{a} \times \vec{b}</m> and has a magnitude equal to the area of the parallelogram formed by <m>\vec{a}</m> and <m>\vec{b}</m>.
      The direction of the cross product is determined by the right-hand rule. 
      Spread your right hand fingers in the direction of <m>\vec{a}</m>, then curl them toward <m>\vec{b}</m>; your thumb points in the direction of <m>\vec{a} \times \vec{b}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      If <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be the two vectors then 
      <md> \vec{a} \times \vec{b} = \vert \vec{a} \vert \vert \vec{b} \vert \sin \theta \vec{n}</md>, 
      where <m>\theta</m> is the angle between <m>\vec{a}</m> and <m>\vec{b}</m> and <m>\vec{n}</m> is a unit vector perpendicular to both <m>\vec{a}</m> and <m>\vec{b}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      If <m>a = \langle a_1, a_2, a_3 \rangle</m> and <m>b = \langle b_1, b_2, b_3 \rangle</m>, then the cross product of
      <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> is the vector
      <md>a \times b = \langle a_2 b_3 - a_3 b_2, a_3 b_1 - a_1 b_3, a_1 b_2 - a_2 b_1 \rangle</md>
    </p>
   
     <p>
      The cross product can be easily computed using the determinant of a square matrix:
      
      <md>
        \vec{a} \times \vec{b} =
        \begin{vmatrix}
          \hat{i} &amp; \hat{j} &amp; \hat{k} \\
          a_1 &amp; a_2 &amp; a_3 \\
          b_1 &amp; b_2 &amp; b_3
        \end{vmatrix}
        = (a_2 b_3 - b_2 a_3)\hat{i}
        - (a_1 b_3 - b_1 a_3)\hat{j}
        + (a_1 b_2 - b_1 a_2)\hat{k}
      </md>
    </p>

<corollary xml:id="cor-parallel_vect">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Two nonzero vectors <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are parallel if and only if
      <m>a \times b = 0</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</corollary>

<note>
  <p> 
    <md>
     \hat{i} \times \hat{j}  = \hat{k} \quad   \hat{j} \times \hat{k}   = \hat{i}  \quad  \hat{k} \times \hat{i} = \hat{j}  \\
    \hat{j} \times \hat{i}   = -\hat{k}  \quad  \hat{k} \times \hat{j}   = -\hat{i} \quad  \hat{i} \times \hat{k}  = -\hat{j} 
  </md>
  </p>
  
</note>


  </introduction>

  <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-scalar_triple-product">
    <title>Scalar Triple Product of Vectors</title>
    <p>
      The scalar triple product of three vectors <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> is the scalar
      <md>a \cdot (b \times c)</md>. It gives the volume of the parallelepiped formed by <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m>. 
      The scalar triple product is also equal to the determinant of the matrix formed by the three vectors as its rows:
      <md>
        a \cdot (b \times c) =
        \begin{vmatrix}
          a_1 &amp; a_2 &amp; a_3 \\
          b_1 &amp; b_2 &amp; b_3 \\
          c_1 &amp; c_2 &amp; c_3
        \end{vmatrix}
      </md>
    </p>
  </subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-vector_triple-product">
  <title>Vector Triple Product of Vectors</title>
  <p>
    The vector triple product of three vectors <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> is the vector
    <md>a \times (b \times c)</md>. It can be computed using the formula:
    <md>
      a \times (b \times c) = (a \cdot c)b - (a \cdot b)c
    </md>
  </p>
</subsubsection>

<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the cross product of the vectors <m>a = \langle 1, 2, 3 \rangle</m> and <m>b = \langle 4, 5, 6 \rangle</m> 
      and verify that it is orthogonal to both vectors.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      The cross product is
      <md>
        a \times b =
        \begin{vmatrix}
          \hat{i} &amp; \hat{j} &amp; \hat{k} \\
          1 &amp; 2 &amp; 3 \\
          4 &amp; 5 &amp; 6
        \end{vmatrix}
        = -3\hat{i} + 6\hat{j} - 3\hat{k}
      </md>
      Thus, <m>a \times b = \langle -3, 6, -3 \rangle</m>. 
      To verify orthogonality, we compute
      <md>
        a \cdot (a \times b) = 1(-3) + 2(6) + 3(-3) = 0
      </md>
      and
      <md>
        b \cdot (a \times b) = 4(-3) + 5(6) + 6(-3) = 0
      </md>
      Therefore, <m>a \times b</m> is orthogonal to both <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Compute the vector triple product <m>a \times (b \times c)</m> for the vectors <m>a = \langle 1, 2, 3 \rangle</m>, <m>b = \langle 4, 5, 6 \rangle</m>, and <m>c = \langle 7, 8, 9 \rangle</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Using the vector triple product formula, we have
      <md>
        a \cdot c = 1(7) + 2(8) + 3(9) = 50
      </md>
      and
      <md>
        a \cdot b = 1(4) + 2(5) + 3(6) = 32
      </md>
      Therefore,
      <md>
        a \times (b \times c) = 50b - 32c
        = 50\langle 4, 5, 6 \rangle - 32\langle 7, 8, 9 \rangle
        = \langle 64, 114, 132 \rangle
      </md>
    </p>
  </solution> 
</example>

<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Compute the scalar triple product <m>a \cdot (b \times c)</m> for the vectors <m>a = \langle 1, 2, 3 \rangle</m>, <m>b = \langle 4, 5, 6 \rangle</m>, and <m>c = \langle 7, 8, 9 \rangle</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      The scalar triple product is
      <md>
        a \cdot (b \times c) =
        \begin{vmatrix}
          1 &amp; 2 &amp; 3 \\
          4 &amp; 5 &amp; 6 \\
          7 &amp; 8 &amp; 9
        \end{vmatrix}
         </md>
         <md>
          = 1(5 \cdot 9 - 6 \cdot 8) - 2(4 \cdot 9 - 6 \cdot 7) + 3(4 \cdot 8 - 5 \cdot 7) = 0
         </md>
         
      Therefore, the vectors <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> are coplanar.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find two unit vectors orthogonal to both <m>a = \langle 3, 2, 1 \rangle</m> and <m>b = \langle -1, 1, 0 \rangle</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      The cross product is
      <md>
        a \times b =
        \begin{vmatrix}
          \hat{i} &amp; \hat{j} &amp; \hat{k} \\
          3 &amp; 2 &amp; 1 \\
          -1 &amp; 1 &amp; 0
        \end{vmatrix}
        = -\hat{i} - \hat{j} + 5\hat{k}
      </md>
      Thus, <m>a \times b = \langle -1, -1, 5 \rangle</m>. The magnitude is
      <md>
        \|a \times b\| = \sqrt{(-1)^2 + (-1)^2 + 5^2} = \sqrt{27} = 3\sqrt{3}
      </md>
      Therefore, the two unit vectors are
      <md>
        \pm \frac{a \times b}{\|a \times b\|}
        = \pm \left\langle -\frac{1}{3\sqrt{3}}, -\frac{1}{3\sqrt{3}}, \frac{5}{3\sqrt{3}} \right\rangle    
      </md>
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the area of the parallelogram with vertices <m>A = (-2, 1)</m>,
      <m>B = (0, 4)</m>, <m>C = (4, 2)</m>, and <m>D = (2, -1)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      The vectors from <m>A</m> to the adjacent vertices are
      <md>
        \vec{AB} = \langle 2, 3, 0 \rangle, \quad
        \vec{AD} = \langle 4, -2, 0 \rangle
      </md>
      The cross product is
      <md>
        \vec{AB} \times \vec{AD} =
        \begin{vmatrix}
          \hat{i} &amp; \hat{j} &amp; \hat{k} \\
          2 &amp; 3 &amp; 0 \\
          4 &amp; -2 &amp; 0
        \end{vmatrix}
        = -16\hat{k}
      </md>
      Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is
      <md>
        \left\|\vec{AB} \times \vec{AD}\right\| = 16
      </md>
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the volume of the parallelepiped with vertices <m>A = (0, 0, 0)</m>,
      <m>B = (1, 0, 0)</m>, <m>C = (0, 1, 0)</m>, and <m>D = (0, 0, 1)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      The vectors from <m>A</m> to the adjacent vertices are
      <md>
        \vec{AB} = \langle 1, 0, 0 \rangle, \quad
        \vec{AC} = \langle 0, 1, 0 \rangle, \quad
        \vec{AD} = \langle 0, 0, 1 \rangle
      </md>
      The volume of the parallelepiped is the absolute value of the scalar triple product:
      <md>
        \left| \vec{AB} \cdot (\vec{AC} \times \vec{AD}) \right| =
        \left| \begin{vmatrix}
          1 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\
          0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 \\
          0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1
        \end{vmatrix} \right| = 1
      </md>
    </p>
  </solution> 
</example>
</subsection>


</section>